  "query_preview": "../Saturday through Tuesday/...Going into Saturday\nmorning, the strong high pressure still looks to keep the front \nfurther westward, not fully pushing into the rest of the \npanhandle until Saturday evening into Saturday night. The timing \nof this has increased in confidence, however remains somewhat \nuncertain for more inland parts of the panhandle from Juneau \nsouthward on if the precipitation onset will be more in the\nevening or pushed later into the overnight hours, largely \ndependent on how quickly the high pressure begins to break down \nand allow for the system to push in further than the outer coast. \nModel agreement is however narrowing in on the timing of this \nfirst front pushing east across the panhandle Saturday evening \nthrough Sunday morning, with the highest rainfall totals expected \nalong the NE Gulf coastline around Yakutat. Generally expecting\naround 2 to 3 inches in 24 hours for the Yakutat area Friday night\nthrough Saturday night.\n\nThe precipitation type expected is still a bit uncertain for the\nSaturday night period and will depend largely on the location, as\nsome parts of the northern panhandle further inland from the \nouter Gulf coast may see cold air damming near the surface \nSaturday night into the overnight hours as the front initially moves\nin. With this in place, some locations at sea level in the \nnorthern panhandle may initially see rain with snow mixing in \nbefore quickly transitioning to rain. Any snow mixing in is low in\nconfidence at this time still, and will not have any expected \naccumulation at sea level.\n\nThis more active pattern will continue into the start of next \nweek as a series of lows move into the Gulf, keeping wetter and \ncomparatively warmer weather in the forecast",
